<Base title="1/4 vs 3/8 quick-connect" description="Tell 1/4 inch and 3/8 inch pressure-washer quick-connects apart by comparing the plug to a colored nozzle tip." path="/qc-14-vs-38/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/quick-connect/">Quick-connect</a> / 1/4 vs 3/8</p>
    <h1>1/4″ vs 3/8″ quick-connect</h1>
    <Answer>
      Hold a colored nozzle tip next to the plug in question. Same size → 1/4″. Obviously fatter → 3/8″. Do not use the hose ID as the only clue.
    </Answer>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>1/4″ QC</th><th>3/8″ QC</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Looks like</td><td>Nozzle tip plug</td><td>Garden-hose-ish steel plug, shorter and thicker</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Typical job</td><td>Tips, foam, small cleaners</td><td>Hose and gun</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cross-fit</td><td>Will not lock in 3/8″</td><td>Will not enter 1/4″</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Some “universal” sockets claim both. They are sloppy at one of the two sizes. For a 3000 PSI gas unit, buy a socket that names one size and a PSI rating.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
